s:
In the end of monosyllables it is sometimes s,
               as in this;
               and sometimes z,
               as in as, has;
               and generally where es stands in verbs for eth,
               as gives.
               It seems to be established as a rule, that no noun singular should end with s single: therefore in words written with diphthongs, and naturally long, an e is nevertheless added at the end,
               as goose, house;
               and where the syllable is short the s is doubled, and was once sse,
               as ass, anciently asse; wilderness, anciently wildernesse; distress, anciently distresse.
